Day 10 – Advanced String Handling & Regular Expressions

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on advanced string handling concepts in Java, including differences between String, StringBuffer, and StringBuilder, along with an introduction to Regular Expressions.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ String vs StringBuffer vs StringBuilder

The mentor explained the key differences based on mutability, performance, and thread-safety.

✅ String

Immutable (cannot be modified once created)

Stored in the String Pool (if created using literals)

Creates a new object whenever modified

✅ StringBuffer

Mutable (can be modified without creating a new object)

Thread-safe (synchronized methods)

Slightly slower due to synchronization

✅ StringBuilder

Mutable

Not thread-safe

Faster than StringBuffer

Preferred when thread-safety is not required

2️⃣ String Pool Concept

String literals are stored in a special memory area called the String Pool.

Duplicate string literals share the same memory reference.

Helps in memory optimization.

Strings created using new keyword are stored in heap memory.

3️⃣ Regular Expressions (Regex)

The session introduced Regular Expressions for pattern matching and text validation.

Key classes discussed:

Pattern class → Used to define regex pattern

Matcher class → Used to match pattern with input text

Applications of Regex:

Email validation

Phone number validation

Password strength checking

Searching and replacing patterns in text

